-- title:
--   Proposition 3.1 — cut and paste does not decrease the aggregated optimal value
-- statement:
--   Let (3.10) satisfy Assumption 3.2. Let $V$ be the optimal value of the fully aggregated problem for the scenario tree $\mathcal T$, and let $V^+$ be the optimal value of the corresponding problem on the tree $\mathcal T^+$ generated from $\mathcal T$ by cut and paste. Then
--   $$
--   V^+\ge V.
--   $$
--
--   So refining the tree by splitting a node never worsens the lower bound of Lemma 3.1.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The optimal values are those of (3.14) with node data computed by (3.13), which is what the steps S1 and S3 assign.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: Assumption 3.2: the A_tt complete fixed recourse matrices ∀t → complete recourse of A_tt for t = 2, …, T; reason: A_11 is not a recourse matrix, and the book's Example 3.1 calls a problem with a non-complete first stage complete fixed recourse; this is the weaker hypothesis, so the statement implies the ∀t one
--   - generalization: supp ℙ_{ξ_t} ⊆ Ξ^t → ξ_t ∈ Ξ^t almost surely; reason: the support inclusion implies the almost sure one, and they agree for closed Ξ^t, so the statement specializes to the source
--   - generalization: (3.18), i.e. (3.14) with p_n > 0 for all nodes → (3.14) on trees that may have null cells; reason: trees with all p_n > 0 are a special case; null nodes carry no weight in the objective
--   - encoding: min → infimum in EReal; reason: +∞ on infeasible and -∞ on unbounded problems; the book assumes neither case away
--   - generalization: a partition of Ξ^{ν+1} into subintervals, |C_n| = r_n ≥ 1 → ScenarioTree, whose cells may be empty (a node may then have no child when Ξ^{ν+1} is empty); reason: every tree of p.261 is a ScenarioTree, so the statement specializes to the book's trees
--   - encoding: ξ̄^n_t = 𝔼[ξ_t | ξ_t ∈ Ξ^t_n], undefined on a P-null cell → xiBar = 0 on a P-null cell (Lean's 0⁻¹ = 0); reason: such a node and its descendants have p_n = 0, and their constraints are solvable by complete recourse of A_tt (t ≥ 2), so no value of (3.14) depends on the choice
-- source:
--   Kall & Mayer, Stochastic Linear Programming: Models, Theory, and Computation (Kluwer/Springer 2005), Chapter 3, Proposition 3.1, p.269 (PDF 278); steps S1–S4, p.268 (PDF 277)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_KallMayerSLP_Multistage_Assumption32
import Definitions.Def_KallMayerSLP_Multistage_aggValue
import Definitions.Def_KallMayerSLP_Multistage_IsCutAndPaste

namespace KallMayerSLP.Multistage

open MeasureTheory

/-- **Chapter 3, Proposition 3.1** (Kall & Mayer, *Stochastic Linear Programming* (2005), Chapter 3,
Proposition 3.1, p.269, PDF 278): "With `V` being the optimal value of the fully aggregated problem
(3.18) corresponding to the scenario tree `𝒯`, and `V⁺` being the optimal value for the corresponding
LP on `𝒯⁺` as generated by cut and paste, it follows that `V⁺ ≥ V`."

**Formalization Note.** The book's (3.18) is (3.14) with all `p_n > 0` (its Assumption 3.1). This
statement does not require that: null cells are allowed in `𝒯` and in `𝒯⁺`. With all `p_n > 0` it is
the book's proposition. -/
theorem proposition_3_1 {D : MSLP} {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {P : Measure Ω}
    [IsProbabilityMeasure P] {ξ : ∀ t, Ω → Fin (D.r t) → ℝ} {Ξ : ∀ t, Set (Fin (D.r t) → ℝ)}
    (hA : Assumption32 D P ξ Ξ) (𝒯 𝒯p : ScenarioTree D Ξ) {s : ℕ} (i : 𝒯.Node s)
    (i₁ i₂ : 𝒯p.Node s) (φ : ∀ t, 𝒯p.Node t → 𝒯.Node t) (hcp : IsCutAndPaste 𝒯 𝒯p i i₁ i₂ φ) :
    aggValue 𝒯 P ξ ≤ aggValue 𝒯p P ξ := by sorry

end KallMayerSLP.Multistage
